## 1. Project Context

### 1.1 What BrandGuard AI Is

BrandGuard AI is an **Enterprise Brand Decision Intelligence Platform (EBDIP)** — a multi-tenant SaaS product that evaluates AI-generated and human-generated marketing content (text, image, video) against an organization's own codified brand identity, and produces an explainable, evidence-traceable verdict on whether that content authentically represents the organization.

It is **not** a content generator, **not** a replacement for human creative judgment, and **not** a generic AI writing/design tool. It is a governance and validation layer that sits between content creation and publication.

It is **organization-agnostic**: any tenant can onboard by supplying its own brand assets. There is no structural dependency on any specific customer, including any hackathon problem-statement source (DR-009).

### 1.2 The Problem It Solves

Generative AI optimizes for fluency and coherence, not brand uniqueness. As organizations lean on AI for marketing content, output drifts toward generic, interchangeable patterns — **Brand Identity Drift**. No existing category of tool (grammar checkers, design tools, general LLMs, CV models, post-publication analytics) answers: *"Does this content authentically represent our brand, why or why not, and what should change?"*

### 1.3 The Core Architectural Commitment (Non-Negotiable)

**Evidence-first decision-making.** This is the platform's actual defensibility, and it governs every implementation decision:

1. AI/ML models extract **structured, confidence-scored Evidence** from content.
2. A **deterministic, versioned Decision function** converts Evidence + Policy into a score and verdict.
3. A language model **narrates** the resulting evidence graph in natural language — it never originates a judgment.

If an implementation approach makes it easier to blur these three steps together — for example, letting a worker's LLM call directly influence a score, or letting a narration prompt see policy weights — that approach is wrong regardless of how much simpler it seems. See §2 and §5.

### 1.4 The Reasoning Pipeline, End to End

```
Organization onboards
  → uploads brand assets (guidelines, logos, design systems, historical campaigns)
  → Brand Genome Compiler produces a draft Genome
  → Human review & sign-off (DR-006 — mandatory, non-negotiable)
  → Genome version activated (immutable snapshot, pointed-to via Brand.active_genome_version_id)

Content uploaded (text / image / video)
  → Applicability Determination (which Genome elements are even in scope for this asset)
  → Planning (which workers must run for the in-scope elements)
  → Worker Execution (per-modality AI workers produce Observations)
  → Evidence Normalization & Fusion (Observations → Evidence, mapped to specific Assertions)
  → Completeness Verification (every in-scope Assertion has a terminal outcome)
  → AnalysisRun reaches `complete` (handoff to Decision Engine) or `failed`

Decision Engine (deterministic, versioned)
  → Evidence Aggregation (Assertion → Component → Category → Overall, confidence-weighted)
  → Policy Evaluation (tenant-specific weights, Critical Rules, verdict thresholds)
  → Decision (score + verdict), reproducible given (Evidence, Genome version, Policy version, decision_function_version)

Recommendation Generation
  → Deterministic trigger + deterministic priority (Policy-driven)
  → LLM narrates the wording only — schema-constrained, evidence-grounded, never free generation

Report (persisted read model)
  → Materializes Decision + Evidence + Recommendations, reachable via analysis_run_id
  → Genome/Policy version pinned permanently (DR-005)
  → Reviewed by a Marketing Manager: approve for publication or return for revision
```

### 1.5 The Six Bounded Contexts

| # | Context | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Identity & Access | Organization, Workspace, User, RoleAssignment, Membership, InternalOperator |
| 2 | Brand Governance | Brand, BrandGenome, Policy, BrandHistory |
| 3 | Campaign & Asset Management | Campaign, Asset, AssetVersion |
| 4 | Analysis & Decision | AnalysisRun, Observation, Evidence, Decision, Recommendation |
| 5 | Reporting | Report (review/flag lifecycle only) |
| 6 | Platform Governance | AuditLogEntry, AISlopKnowledgeBase |

No entity spans two contexts. No context spans a tenant-isolation boundary. Module structure in code should mirror this (§6).

### 1.6 Multi-Tenancy in One Sentence

Every tenant-scoped entity carries a denormalized `org_id`, traces back to exactly one Organization, and no read or write may ever resolve using another tenant's identifier — enforced independently at the domain, access, execution, and observability layers (defense in depth, not a single filter).

### 1.7 Why Explainability Is Structural, Not a UI Feature

A user must never be able to see a score without being one click away from the Evidence behind it. This is possible because the entire aggregation tree is **derived, not stored** — it is always exactly reconstructable from (Evidence set, pinned Genome version, pinned Policy version, `decision_function_version`). The product surface's job is to expose that reconstruction, never to compute anything new on its own.

