## 12. Recommended Hooks

| Hook | Trigger | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Run tests | After any file edit affecting `backend/src/` or `workers/` | Catch regressions immediately, not at commit time |
| Run `ruff` format + lint | Pre-commit | Enforce formatting/lint consistency automatically |
| Run `mypy` | Pre-commit | Catch type errors before they reach CI |
| Secret scan (`gitleaks`) | Pre-commit | Block any commit containing an apparent credential |
| Architecture-invariant check | Pre-commit (custom script) | Grep-based or AST-based check for known violation patterns — e.g., a worker module importing anything from `decision_engine/`, or an LLM client import inside `decision_engine/` — fails the commit if found |
| Tenant-isolation test gate | Pre-push / CI | Require the `tests/tenant_isolation/` suite to pass before any push to a shared branch |
| Property test gate (Hypothesis) | CI | Require the Decision Engine's reproducibility property tests to pass before merge |
| Security scan (`bandit`, `semgrep`) | CI | Standard static analysis on every PR |
| Dependency audit (`pip-audit`/Dependabot) | Scheduled + CI | Flag known-vulnerable dependencies |

The **architecture-invariant check** hook is the most project-specific and highest-value one to build early: a lightweight static check that fails the commit if, for example, an LLM/API client import appears inside `decision_engine/`, or a worker module imports across a Bounded Context boundary directly rather than through its declared interface. This turns several of §2's invariants into something enforced mechanically, not just documented.

